[TH]Gate[/TH]
[TH]Number it enforces[/TH]
[TH]Where it runs[/TH]
[/TR]
[TR]
[TD]Automated test suite[/TD]
[TD]651 committed JUnit test classes across the modules; [I]rtp-core[/I] alone carries roughly 3,700 test cases[/TD]
[TD][I]gradle.yml[/I] on every push and pull request[/TD]
[/TR]
[TR]
[TD]Mutation testing (PIT)[/TD]
[TD]>= 60 % mutation score required on safety packages; measured 83 % (world border), 85 % (vertical adjustors), 81 % (region cache), 77 % (memory table), 61 % (teleport pipeline)[/TD]
[TD][I]mutation-testing.yml[/I][/TD]
[/TR]
[TR]
[TD]Prohibition enforcement (ArchUnit)[/TD]
[TD]10 rules, one or more per safety prohibition S-001 through S-007 - no unsafe destinations, no permanently force-loaded chunks, no claim bypass, no swallowed failures, no main-thread chunk I/O[/TD]
[TD][I]gradle.yml[/I][/TD]
[/TR]
[TR]
[TD]Changed-line coverage[/TD]
[TD]new and modified lines must be >= 80 % covered, or the run fails[/TD]
[TD][I]gradle.yml[/I] ([I]scripts/diff-coverage.py[/I])[/TD]
[/TR]
[TR]
[TD]Static analysis[/TD]
[TD]SpotBugs plus a PMD ruleset that includes a project-specific [I]PreferNonLockingExecution[/I] rule, so blocking constructs are flagged during review[/TD]
[TD][I]gradle.yml[/I] ([I]-PstaticAnalysis[/I])[/TD]
[/TR]
[TR]
[TD]Binary API compatibility[/TD]
[TD]all 7 public API modules compared against the last release; an unannounced break fails the build, and removals must clear a 2-minor deprecation window[/TD]
[TD][I]gradle.yml[/I] (japicmp)[/TD]
[/TR]
[TR]
[TD]Dependency CVEs[/TD]
[TD]OWASP dependency-check fails at CVSS >= 4; every accepted finding needs written justification in the suppression file[/TD]
[TD][I]dependency-check.yml[/I], weekly and on dependency changes[/TD]
[/TR]
[TR]
[TD]Release provenance[/TD]
[TD]every release ships a CycloneDX SBOM, SHA-256 and SHA-512 checksums, GPG signatures, SLSA build provenance, and the acceptance-run evidence bundle[/TD]
[TD][I]release.yml[/I][/TD]
[/TR]
[TR]
[TD]Runtime acceptance[/TD]
[TD]nightly multi-server devstack: 2 Velocity proxies, 2 lobbies, 2 backends over Redis, exercising Velocity + Paper + Folia + Fabric on Java 21 LTS and 25+[/TD]
[TD][I]devstack-acceptance.yml[/I][/TD]
[/TR]
[TR]
[TD]Encoding and docs hygiene[/TD]
[TD]tracked text files scanned for UTF-8 mojibake; the docs site builds from the same tree you read[/TD]
[TD][I]gradle.yml[/I], [I]docs.yml[/I][/TD]
[/TR]
[/TABLE]

[B]What I haven't finished.[/B] [I]rtp-core[/I] and [I]rtp-proxy-common[/I] are still under the 90 / 80 target on branch coverage. Their floors sit a point or two below the measured numbers, because a partially recompiled tree makes JaCoCo discard stale execution data and read low; the floors only ever ratchet upward. What's left uncovered in [I]rtp-core[/I] is concentrated in the platform bootstrap and dispatch seams a plain-JVM test can't reach honestly, so I'm closing those with runtime-attested coverage from the nightly devstack instead of writing tests that only exercise mocks. Spigot, NeoForge, BungeeCord, and non-LTS Java releases run fine but have no scheduled live CI suite, so the support matrix lists them as best-effort.

[B]Scope of these numbers.[/B] Coverage and mutation scores measure how thoroughly the tests exercise the code. They are not a bug-free claim, and they say nothing about feature correctness on your world, your generator, or your plugin list; the nightly devstack run and your own staging server cover that.

